-- title:
--   j(q) is integral over K[j(qᵖ)]
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $p$ be a prime. Inside the field of Laurent series `LaurentSeries K` consider the element [`ModularCurve.jqModC K`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15), namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K$ of the power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$-type numerator (`eisenstein4 ^ 3 * dedekindEtaUnitInv`), i.e. the $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant; and [`ModularCurve.jqNModC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), its image under the ring homomorphism `qExpand K p`, which multiplies all Hahn-series exponents by $p$, i.e. the substitution $q \mapsto q^p$. Let [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L61) be the intermediate field $K(\,j(q),\,j(q^p)\,)$ of `LaurentSeries K` obtained by adjoining these two elements to $K$. The theorem asserts that the element of this intermediate field given by [`ModularCurve.jqModC K`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) (together with its membership witness) is integral over the $K$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin K` generated by the single element [`ModularCurve.jqNModC K p`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18): that is, it satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in $K[j(q^p)]$. No hypotheses beyond $K$ being a field and $p$ being prime are imposed.
--
--   This is the integrality half of the classical modular equation of level $p$: the modular polynomial $\Phi_p(X,Y)$ is monic in each variable, so $j(q)$ is integral over $K[j(q^p)]$ in the level-$p$ function field. It is used in the construction of Katz-style forms on $\Gamma_0$, being cited by [`ModularForm.exists_katzGamma0Form_evalCusp_eq_of_five_le`](thm.html#ModularForm.exists_katzGamma0Form_evalCusp_eq_of_five_le).
-- source:
